# Working on {{.Title}}

Guidance for coding agents. This is a **Gleam** web app on the **Howdy**
framework, release {{.Release}}, running on the BEAM (Erlang). Gleam is
statically typed with no exceptions, nulls or mutation: functions return
`Result`, `use` flattens callbacks, and values are piped with `|>`. Howdy is
young and not in your training data, so read the code already in this
project before writing new code, and copy its patterns.
{{- if .API}}

The project is **API only**: a JSON backend with no pages, layouts, Howdy UI
or static files.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Database}}

Its database is **{{if eq .Database "postgres"}}PostgreSQL{{else}}SQLite{{end}}**, used through Gloo{{if eq .Database "postgres"}} and pog{{end}}.
{{- end}}

## Task guides

Step-by-step guides for common tasks, with the Howdy APIs they need, are
in `.claude/skills/` (Claude Code loads them as skills). Read the one for
your task before starting:

| Guide | For |
| --- | --- |
| `howdy-feature/SKILL.md` | building a new feature end to end |
| `howdy-controllers/SKILL.md` | routes, requests, responses, errors, middleware |
{{- if .Database}}
| `howdy-database/SKILL.md` | schema changes, migrations and queries |
{{- end}}
{{- if not .API}}
| `howdy-pages/SKILL.md` | pages, Howdy UI, forms, live views, styles |
{{- end}}
| `howdy-testing/SKILL.md` | writing and fixing tests |
| `howdy-debugging/SKILL.md` | finding out why something is broken |

## Check your work

Run this after every change, and fix what it reports before moving on:

```sh
howdy check          # gleam format --check, gleam build, deps, gleam test
```

Add `--json` for a result you can parse. The steps on their own are
`gleam format src test dev`, `gleam build` and `gleam test`. `deps` fails
when `src/` imports a package that only a dev dependency brings in:
`gleam build` accepts it, but a production build leaves it out.
{{- if eq .Database "postgres"}}
The tests need PostgreSQL: start it with `docker compose up -d`.
{{- end}}

`howdy routes` lists every route the app serves, with the file and line it
is defined on (`--json` too).

## Generate code rather than writing it

The `howdy` CLI writes new code in the project's shape, with tests, and
registers it in the app. Use it before writing a feature by hand, and read
what it wrote. Pass `--dry-run` to see which files a command would create or
change without writing anything, and `--json` for output you can parse; a
`manual_steps` list in the result says what it could not wire up itself.

| Command | Writes |
| --- | --- |
{{- if .Database}}
| `howdy create controller <plural> <singular> name:type ...` | a feature with a record, database operations, a JSON API under `/api/<plural>`, a migration and tests |
{{- if not .API}}
| `howdy create pages <plural> <singular> name:type ...` | the same with HTML pages to list, show, add, edit and delete (`--api` adds the JSON API too) |
{{- end}}
| `howdy create repo <plural> <singular> name:type ...` | a record, database operations and migration, without routes |
| `howdy create migration <table> name:type ...` | a migration for a new table |
{{- end}}
{{- if not .API}}
| `howdy create page <controller> <page>` | a page, added to the controller (created if needed) |
| `howdy create live_page <controller> <page>` | a page with a live Lustre server component |
| `howdy create component [controller] <name>` | a reusable piece of view |
| `howdy create live_component [controller] <name>` | a live server component with its socket routed |
| `howdy create layout <name>` | a layout for pages to render through (`--layout <name>`) |
{{- end}}
| `howdy add mail` | email, with `howdy create email <name>` for each one |
{{- if .Database}}
| `howdy add auth` | sign-in, registration and roles; then `--add-auth` on `create` guards routes |
| `howdy add flags` | feature flags kept in the database |
{{- end}}
| `howdy add openapi` | an OpenAPI document at `/openapi.json` and a reference at `/docs` |
| `howdy add remote` | typed calls between services |
{{- if .Database}}

Field types are `string`, `int`, `float` and `bool`; every record also gets
an integer `id`.
{{- end}}
Run `howdy <command> --help` for the details of each.

## Where things go

- `src/{{.Name}}.gleam` is the composition root: the `Services` the app is
  built from, and `app`, which registers every controller. A new controller
  is registered here, ahead of {{if .API}}the `howdy.not_found` handler{{else}}the static catch-all{{end}}.
- `src/{{.Name}}/features/<feature>/` holds one feature: its controller
  (`<feature>_controller.gleam`){{if .Database}}, record (`<singular>.gleam`: type,
  validation, JSON codecs, Gloo table), database operations
  (`<plural>.gleam`){{end}}{{if not .API}}, `pages/` and `components/`{{end}}. Put new
  features in a folder of their own here.
- The other folders under `src/{{.Name}}/` belong to Howdy's generators:
  {{if not .API}}`layouts/`, `components/`, {{end}}{{if .Database}}`migrations/`, {{end}}`platform/` (framework wiring).
- `test/{{.Name}}/` mirrors `src/`. `test/{{.Name}}/support.gleam` builds the
  app for tests{{if .Database}}, and `support.with_db` gives each test a fresh database{{end}}.
- `dev/{{.Name}}_dev.gleam` is the `gleam dev` entry point{{if .Full}}, which also mounts the admin{{end}}.
{{- if .Database}}
- `src/tasks/` holds `gleam run -m tasks/<name>` scripts, such as `tasks/migrate`.
{{- end}}
{{- if not .API}}
- `priv/public/` is served as static files. `priv/public/assets/style.css`
  is yours; `ui.css` is generated by `gleam run -m tasks/css`.
{{- end}}

## Conventions

- A controller is `controller.new("prefix")` piped into routes such as
  `|> controller.get("/:id", fn(ctx) { ... })`; read path parameters with
  `use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")`. Put fixed routes such as `/new` before
  ones with a parameter such as `/:id`.
{{- if .Database}}
- Database access goes through the feature's operations module, never from a
  controller directly. Queries use Gloo, with the connection (`db: Repo`)
  passed in from the services.
- Never edit a migration that may have run. Change the schema with a new
  migration (`howdy create migration`, or a file with the next version) and
  list it in `src/{{.Name}}/platform/database.gleam`. Migrations run when the
  app starts, or with `gleam run -m tasks/migrate`.
{{- end}}
{{- if not .API}}
- Pages are built from Howdy UI (`howdy/ui`) on the server and render through
  a layout in `src/{{.Name}}/layouts/`: `default_layout.render(ctx, title, content)`,
  or `render_live` for a page with live sections.
{{- end}}
- Tests send requests through the app without a socket, with `howdy/testing`:
  `testing.get("/path") |> testing.send(app)`. Add a test beside the code you
  change; `gleam test` runs every function ending in `_test`.
- Keep modules small and named for what they hold. Doc comments are `///`
  on a function and `////` at the top of a module.

## Running it

```sh
gleam dev            # hot reload at http://127.0.0.1:8787
gleam run            # without hot reload
```
{{- if .Full}}

Under `gleam dev` the Howdy admin at http://127.0.0.1:8787/_howdy shows
{{if .Database}}the database and {{end}}every request as a trace of its {{if .Database}}queries and {{end}}logs: check it
when a request fails.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Database}}
The {{if eq .Database "postgres"}}database is `DATABASE_URL`{{else}}database is `{{.Name}}.sqlite3`{{end}}; configuration is read from the
environment and `.env` (see `.env.example`).
{{- else}}
Configuration is read from the environment and `.env` (see `.env.example`).
{{- end}}

## Upgrading Howdy

Every Howdy package in `gleam.toml` uses the tag `v{{.Release}}`, and they
must stay on one release. Do not change one without the others. See
README.md for the rest of the project's documentation.
